{%- comment -%}
  ss-one-lookup

  One shop.metaobjects.custom_shop[handle] per store, for the whole page.

  This section used to look each store up four separate times — once to sort
  it, once to add up product counts, once to gather logo URLs for the duplicate
  check, and once to draw its card; five for a super admin. Shopify caps how
  many metaobjects one request may read, so a dashboard with enough stores
  spends that budget partway down its own list and every lookup after it comes
  back empty.

  That is the whole family of bugs this section has been patched for. A card
  with no metaobject has no is_fully_ready, so it renders as "building" — which
  set the ready-poll reloading the page every ten seconds, forever, on exactly
  the accounts with more than ten stores. It has no name either, which is why
  cards showed a prettified handle, and why the identity check that was meant
  to stop them showing a NEIGHBOUR's name could not help: nothing was returned
  to check.

  So every store is read once, here, into a record; everything downstream reads
  the record. Same output, a quarter of the budget, and the identity check now
  lives in one place instead of five.

  The records are built into the same three buckets the handles are sorted
  into, so concatenating them yields records already in display order and the
  card loop walks them directly instead of re-deriving anything.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Field and record separators. Chosen so that neither contains the other:
  splitting a record on '~;~' cannot bite into a '~;;~' boundary. The one
  free-text field, the store's name, has both stripped out of it at capture.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign ss_fs = '~;~' -%}
{%- assign ss_rs = '~;;~' -%}

{% assign bucket_admin = '' %}
{% assign bucket_live = '' %}
{% assign bucket_building = '' %}
{% assign rec_admin = '' %}
{% assign rec_live = '' %}
{% assign rec_building = '' %}
{% assign all_clean_urls = '' %}
{% assign total_products_all = 0 %}

{% assign raw_handles = member_handles_blob | split: '||' %}

{% for h in raw_handles %}
  {% assign handle = h | strip %}
  {% if handle == blank %}{% continue %}{% endif %}
  {% capture needle %}||{{ handle }}||{% endcapture %}

  {% assign entry = shop.metaobjects.custom_shop[handle] %}
  {%- comment -%}
    ss-metaobject-identity

    The one lookup is checked against the handle it was asked for, and thrown
    away only when it is POSITIVELY a different store. A card that cannot
    resolve its store should fall back to its own collection; what it must
    never do is wear another store's name, logo and product count, which all
    come from `entry`.

    An earlier version compared the two raw and discarded on any difference,
    including one it could not see: when system.handle reads back blank,
    blank != handle, so every store was discarded and the whole dashboard
    rendered as "building". Discarding needs evidence; the absence of evidence
    keeps the entry.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if entry -%}{%- assign ss_got = entry.system.handle | default: '' | strip | downcase -%}{%- assign ss_want = handle | downcase -%}{%- if ss_got != blank and ss_got != ss_want -%}{%- assign entry = false -%}{%- endif -%}{%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Reset every field before it is read. Liquid has no block scope — one `for`
    body shares a single variable space across iterations — so a path that
    reads a field without first writing it reads the PREVIOUS store's value.
    That is the exact shape of the logo bug this section was patched for twice:
    a run of consecutive cards wearing the mark of the last store above them
    that had one.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Whether the lookup came back at all, kept as its own field.

    Two different ceilings produce an identical-looking card, and they want
    opposite fixes. If Shopify stopped resolving the logo FILE reference, the
    metaobject is here and a text URL fixes it. If Shopify stopped returning
    the METAOBJECT, there is nothing to read a text field out of and the fix
    has to be fewer stores per request instead. From the outside both give a
    card with no logo, a name (from the collection), and a count (also from
    the collection) — so nothing visible separates them. This does.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% assign r_resolved = '0' %}
  {% if entry %}{% assign r_resolved = '1' %}{% endif %}

  {% assign r_ready = '0' %}
  {% assign r_collection = handle %}
  {% assign r_status = '' %}
  {% assign r_clean = '' %}
  {% assign r_logo = '' %}
  {% assign r_logo_big = '' %}
  {% assign r_name = '' %}

  {% if entry %}
    {% assign is_ready = entry.is_fully_ready.value | default: entry.is_fully_ready %}
    {% if is_ready == true or is_ready == 'true' %}{% assign r_ready = '1' %}{% endif %}

    {% assign ch = entry.collection_handle.value | default: entry.collection_handle %}
    {% if ch != blank %}{% assign r_collection = ch | strip %}{% endif %}

    {% assign r_status = entry.status.value | default: entry.status | default: '' | strip | downcase %}

    {% assign n = entry.name.value | default: entry.name %}
    {% if n != blank %}{% assign r_name = n | replace: ss_rs, ' ' | replace: ss_fs, ' ' | strip %}{% endif %}

    {%- comment -%}
      ss-logo-budget

      A text field first, because resolving the logo FILE reference is
      rationed and reading a string is not.

      Shopify resolves at most twenty metaobject file references per request.
      Measured on a real dashboard: cards 1-20 drew their logo, cards 21-36
      drew nothing at all, on one page, with the break falling between two
      alphabetically adjacent stores. Nothing in this section can raise that
      ceiling — every card that resolves entry.logo spends one of the twenty,
      and a seller with more than twenty stores runs out.

      logo_url holds the same picture's CDN URL as plain text, written at
      provisioning where the URL is already in hand. Reading it costs nothing,
      so a store that has it never spends from the budget — which leaves the
      twenty for the stores that do not, instead of the first twenty in
      alphabetical order.

      logo_clean_url is the older background-removed copy and stays as a last
      resort. Both go through the duplicate check below: a URL two stores share
      is not either store's logo, and trusting a shared one blindly is what put
      one club's blue logo on every trailing card.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% assign lcu = entry.logo_url.value | default: entry.logo_url %}
    {% if lcu == blank %}{% assign lcu = entry.logo_clean_url.value | default: entry.logo_clean_url %}{% endif %}
    {% if lcu == blank %}{% assign lcu = entry.logo_clean.value | default: entry.logo_clean %}{% endif %}
    {% if lcu == blank %}{% assign lcu = entry.logo_clean_url_.value | default: entry.logo_clean_url_ %}{% endif %}
    {% if lcu != blank %}
      {% assign r_clean = lcu | strip %}
      {% assign all_clean_urls = all_clean_urls | append: '|' | append: r_clean %}
    {% endif %}

    {%- comment -%}
      `.width` rather than `!= blank`. A metaobject file field that exists but
      holds nothing still returns an object, and an object is never blank, so
      `!= blank` passes for a store with no logo and hands image_url something
      it cannot render. Asking for a property only a real image has is the
      difference between "there is a field here" and "there is a picture here".

      Resolved to a URL now, while the media object is in hand, because a
      record is a string and cannot carry the object itself.

      ONE size, not two. This used to resolve 512 for the card frame and 1200
      for the onboarding image, which is two image resolutions per store on a
      page that renders every store the seller has. Both readers of the
      onboarding attribute (the onboarding modal here, and
      logo-source-report.liquid) already fall back to the card's own <img src>
      when it is empty, so the second size bought a slightly larger picture in
      one modal at the cost of doubling the per-store image work on every load.
      512 covers the largest frame at 3x and is plenty for the modal.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      preview_image.width as well as width.

      A metaobject file reference resolves to a MediaImage, and a MediaImage
      keeps its dimensions on preview_image — plain .width is an image object's
      property, not a media object's. Testing only .width therefore says "no
      picture here" for every store whose logo is a file reference, which left
      the clean URL as the only logo the dashboard could ever draw. When that
      URL was shared between stores the duplicate check correctly dropped it,
      and the card fell through to its initials with a perfectly good logo
      sitting unused on the metaobject.

      Both are accepted, so whichever shape the field resolves to is found.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Only when no text URL was found — see ss-logo-budget. This is the
      rationed one, so it is spent on the stores that have nothing else.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% if r_clean == blank %}
      {% assign lf = entry.logo.value | default: entry.logo %}
      {% if lf.width or lf.preview_image.width %}
        {% assign r_logo = lf | image_url: width: 512 %}
        {% assign r_logo_big = r_logo %}
      {% endif %}
    {% endif %}
  {% endif %}

  {%- comment -%}
    The store's own collection, read once for both the product count and the
    name fallback. collections[] is a separate lookup from the metaobject one
    and its title is the store's name, so a card whose metaobject did not
    resolve still shows what the store is actually called rather than a
    prettified handle — and never another store's name.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% assign c = collections[r_collection] %}
  {% assign r_products = c.products_count | default: 0 %}
  {% assign total_products_all = total_products_all | plus: r_products %}
  {% if r_name == blank and c.title != blank %}{% assign r_name = c.title | replace: ss_rs, ' ' | replace: ss_fs, ' ' | strip %}{% endif %}

  {%- comment -%}
    A constant last field. Liquid's split drops trailing empty segments, so a
    store with no name would otherwise come back one field short and every
    read past it would slide by one.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- capture record -%}{{ handle }}{{ ss_fs }}{{ r_ready }}{{ ss_fs }}{{ r_collection }}{{ ss_fs }}{{ r_products }}{{ ss_fs }}{{ r_status }}{{ ss_fs }}{{ r_clean }}{{ ss_fs }}{{ r_logo }}{{ ss_fs }}{{ r_logo_big }}{{ ss_fs }}{{ r_name }}{{ ss_fs }}{{ r_resolved }}{{ ss_fs }}end{{ ss_rs }}{%- endcapture -%}

  {% if admin_handles_blob contains needle %}
    {% assign bucket_admin = bucket_admin | append: needle %}
    {% assign rec_admin = rec_admin | append: record %}
  {% elsif r_ready == '1' %}
    {% assign bucket_live = bucket_live | append: needle %}
    {% assign rec_live = rec_live | append: record %}
  {% else %}
    {% assign bucket_building = bucket_building | append: needle %}
    {% assign rec_building = rec_building | append: record %}
  {% endif %}
{% endfor %}

{% assign sorted_blob = bucket_admin | append: bucket_live | append: bucket_building %}
{% assign handles_array = sorted_blob | split: '||' %}
{% assign store_records = rec_admin | append: rec_live | append: rec_building | split: ss_rs %}

{%- comment -%}
  Trailing separator so the last URL is never at the very end of the string:
  Liquid's split drops trailing empty segments, so without it a duplicate whose
  second occurrence is last counts as unique — and the final store keeps the
  wrong logo, which is exactly the store that had it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% assign all_clean_urls = all_clean_urls | append: '|' %}

{% if is_god %}
<script>
  window.SS_GOD_STORES = [
    {%- comment -%} From the records built above: see ss-one-lookup. {%- endcomment -%}
    {% for rec in store_records %}
      {% if rec != blank %}
        {% assign f = rec | split: ss_fs %}
        {% assign g_handle = f[0] %}
        {% if g_handle != blank %}
          {% assign g_name = f[8] | default: g_handle %}
          {% if g_name == blank %}{% assign g_name = g_handle %}{% endif %}
          { handle: {{ g_handle | json }}, name: {{ g_name | json }} },
        {% endif %}
      {% endif %}
    {% endfor %}
  ];
  window.SS_ALL_PLATFORM_STORES = [
    {% for store in shop.metaobjects.custom_shop.values %}
      {% unless store.tags contains 'status:closed' %}
        {
          handle: {{ store.system.handle | json }},
          name: {{ store.name | json }},
          collection_handle: {{ store.collection_handle | json }}
        },
      {% endunless %}
    {% endfor %}
  ];
</script>
{% endif %}

<section class="ss-portal" data-ss-portal>
  <div class="ss-bg">
    <div class="ss-wrap">

      {%- comment -%}
        Shown here because this page is where "Create a New Storefront" is
        clicked from. Renders nothing while product building is running.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {% render 'service-status-banner' %}

      <header class="ss-header">
        <div class="ss-header-text">
          <h1 class="ss-h1">
            {% if customer %}
              {% if customer.first_name != blank %}
                {{ customer.first_name | escape }}’s Dashboard
              {% elsif display_name != blank %}
                {{ display_name | escape }}’s Dashboard
              {% else %}
                My Dashboard
              {% endif %}
            {% else %}
              Studio Dashboard
            {% endif %}
