                        </div>
                        <div class="ss-fr-progress" data-ss-fr-progress style="display:none;">
                          <div class="ss-fr-bar"><div class="ss-fr-bar-fill" data-ss-fr-fill></div></div>
                          <div class="ss-fr-meta"><span data-ss-fr-raised></span><span data-ss-fr-goal></span></div>
                        </div>
                      </div>

                    </div>

                  {% endif %}
                {% endfor %}

                {% render 'logo-frame-contrast' %}
                {% render 'logo-source-report' %}
              </div>

              {%- comment -%}
                The pager is markup, not a fetch. Every card is already in the
                page — this only decides which are displayed — so "show more"
                is instant and needs no backend. What it buys is layout and
                paint: the browser was styling and laying out every card in the
                account on first load, and lazily loading the hidden logos
                (above) means their images are not requested either.

                Rendered only when it has something to reveal, and hidden
                entirely for browsers with no JS, where every card is visible
                anyway and a dead button would be a lie.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {% if ss_paging_on %}
